[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Over-Engineering vs Pragmatism: When SOLID Goes Too Far"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to SOLID Principles and Pragmatism"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The SOLID principles are foundational guidelines for writing maintainable and scalable object-oriented software. However, blindly applying every principle to every class often leads to over-engineering, unnecessary abstraction, and plummeting developer productivity. Pragmatism dictates that design patterns should solve actual problems, not hypothetical future requirements."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Trap of Premature Abstraction"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common anti-pattern occurs when developers create intricate hierarchies of interfaces and factories for simple features under the guise of adhering to the Open-Closed Principle (OCP) or Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP). Let us look at an example of an over-engineered notification service."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"interface MessageSender { void send(String message); }
class EmailSender implements MessageSender { public void send(String msg) { ... } }
interface MessageFormatter { String format(String msg); }
interface NotificationFactory { MessageSender createSender(); MessageFormatter createFormatter(); }
// This is often too much boilerplate for a simple email notification system."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Signs You Are Over-Engineering"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"You have single-implementation interfaces that never change."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adding a simple field requires modifying four or more classes or factories."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"New team members spend weeks trying to understand the architectural abstractions rather than the business logic."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"A Pragmatic Alternative"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pragmatic software engineering embraces the KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid) and YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It) principles. Instead of abstracting everything upfront, write straightforward code and refactor toward SOLID principles only when concrete pain points or extension requirements emerge."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"class NotificationService {
    public void sendEmail(String recipient, String message) {
        // Direct, readable, and easy to test without complex DI containers or factories
        System.out.println("Sending email to " + recipient);
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"SOLID principles are tools, not religious dogmas. Use them to manage inevitable complexity, but avoid introducing complexity just to use them. Always let real business value and maintenance friction dictate your architectural decisions."}]
